// case: call-argument-type-unchecked
// what: `takesInt` is declared `fn takesInt(x: int)`, and it is called with a
//      string. The front end knows the parameter type and the argument type and
//      checks neither, so the string's address arrives in an int register and
//      the program prints it: -619324952 on one run, 1436322280 on the next.
//      The number is a heap address, so nothing is pinned about its VALUE --
//      what is pinned is that the call is still accepted, because the day it
//      stops being accepted this case fires.
//
//      This is n14 (a model constructor given the wrong argument types) at the
//      primitive level, and it is the shape that costs the most: an int where a
//      string was meant is a garbage number, not a crash, so it propagates.
//      Two neighbours measured the same day, same root:
//
//        fn f(a: A) -> int   called as f(B(3))  -> prints 3, accepted
//        var n = 0  n = "a string"             -> SIGSEGV, check clean
//
//      FINDINGS F-0015.
// known-defect: F-0015
fn takesInt(x: int) -> int { return x + 1 }

fn main() -> int {
    print(takesInt("a string"))
    return 0
}
